WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
After core helium runs out, a Sun-like star

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.100
has a carbon and oxygen core surrounded by

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.820
burning shells. Helium burns closer to the core,

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.500
with hydrogen burning farther out. The huge, cool

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.940
envelope makes this an asymptotic giant branch star.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.920
A cool surface does not mean a cool

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:16.400
interior.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.980
Hydrogen burning supplies fresh helium. Helium shell burning

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.480
can become unstable, producing thermal pulses. The shells

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.280
change their activity while the envelope expands and

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.220
readjusts. This is different from the earlier helium

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.480
flash that began helium burning in the core.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.620
The location of the burning matters as much

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:35.260
as the fuel.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.820
Helium fusion makes carbon. Deep convection can carry

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.000
processed material toward the surface, and strong winds

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.020
carry it into space. Follow that chain: production,

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.260
mixing, then loss. The lecture emphasizes these giant

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.680
stars as a major source of the galaxy’s

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.540
carbon. Their discarded material can become part of

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.960
later stars and planets.
